              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "Despite his name, **Flounder is not actually a flounder** (real flounders are flat, brown, camouflage-patterned fish that dwell on the ocean floor). \n\nInstead, Flounder's exact species depends on which version of *The Little Mermaid* you are looking at:\n\n### 1. The 1989 Animated Film (Stylized Reef Fish / Angelfish)\nIn the original classic, the animators never officially designated a specific, real-world species for Flounder. He was designed as a vibrant, heavily stylized cartoon tropical fish. Most marine biologists and Disney animators agree that his round, colorful appearance most closely resembles a **blue angelfish** or another type of tropical reef fish.\n\n### 2. The 2023 Live-Action Movie (Sergeant Major Fish)\nFor the photorealistic 2023 live-action adaptation, Disney had to choose a real-world fish to model CGI Flounder after. They officially based his design on a **Sergeant Major** (*Abudefduf saxatilis*). This is a species of tropical damselfish known for its round body, yellow-to-silver coloring, and distinctive vertical dark stripes. \n\n### 3. *The Little Mermaid* Prequel TV Series (A \"Guppy\")\nIn the 1990s animated spin-off television series, Disney added a bit of in-universe lore to Flounder's background. According to the show, Flounder's actual birth name is **\"Guppy Number 35\"**. While real-world guppies are tiny freshwater fish and Flounder lives in the saltwater ocean, \"guppy\" is also used frequently in the franchise as a slang term for being a young, easily-frightened \"scaredy-cat\" fish (such as when Ariel teases him, *\"Don't be such a guppy!\"*).",
